PROMPT_CASE_RUN review status=Completed error=None result=Some("Review complete. Both delegated findings verified directly against the file (`src/limit.rs`, 4 lines total).\n\n## Findings\n\n**1. High — `first_lines` panics on any input shorter than 4 lines** (`src/limit.rs:3`)\n\n```rust\n(0..=MAX_LINES).map(|i| lines[i].to_string()).collect()\n```\n\nThe range `0..=MAX_LINES` is inclusive, yielding indices `0,1,2,3`, and `lines[i]` is unchecked indexing. Any slice with fewer than 4 elements — including the empty slice, and the *normal* case of exactly 3 lines — panics with `index out of bounds`. There is no `get`, bounds check, or error return; a repo-wide grep found no callers or caller-side guarantee in the workspace.\n\n**2. High — Off-by-one: returns `MAX_LINES + 1` elements** (`src/limit.rs:3` with `MAX_LINES = 3` at `src/limit.rs:1`)\n\nFor inputs with ≥ 4 lines, e.g. `[\"a\",\"b\",\"c\",\"d\"]`, the function returns 4 strings despite `MAX_LINES = 3` and the names `MAX_LINES`/`first_lines` implying a maximum of 3. The inclusive `..=` treats the constant as a last-index rather than a count.\n\n**Smallest fix (resolves both):** `src/limit.rs:3` → `(0..MAX_LINES.min(lines.len())).map(|i| lines[i].to_string()).collect()` (or `lines.iter().take(MAX_LINES).map(|s| s.to_string()).collect()`). No files were changed.\n\n## Verified correct\n\n- No unsafe code, no `unwrap`/`expect`, no integer overflow possible (all values are small `usize`), deterministic and pure given input.\n\n## Coverage limits\n\n- `code_diagnostics` could not run (rust-analyzer warning-health failure), so a clean compile is unconfirmed; both findings are source-level.\n- No docs, tests, or callers exist to disambiguate intent, so finding 2's severity rests on the identifier names as the contract.")
