t URI or child run session/events if exact omitted content is needed.\n# Code Review — `src/limit.rs` (correctness, read-only)\n\n## Findings\n\n### 1. `first_lines` panics (index out of bounds) on any input shorter than the maximum — **high**\n\n**Evidence:** `src/limit.rs:1-4`\n\n```rust\npub const MAX_LINES: usize = 3;\npub fn first_lines(lines: &[&str]) -> Vec<String> {\n    (0..=MAX_LINES).map(|i| lines[i].to_string()).collect()\n}\n```\n\n**Failure condition:** `(0..=MAX_LINES)` is an *inclusive* range, so it yields `0, 1, 2, 3` — 4 iterations. `lines[i]` is unchecked indexing, so the function panics with `index out of bounds: the len is N but the index is 3` whenever `lines.len() < 4`. Given the name (`MAX_LINES = 3`) and the contract implied by the name `first_lines`, the intended output is the first 3 lines, so the panic condition is the *normal* case: any 3-line or shorter input (including the empty slice) panics. Only inputs of 4+ lines return without panicking — and those return one line too many (finding 2). There is no bounds check, `get`, error return, or caller-side guarantee anywhere in this workspace (no other `.rs` files exist; `first_lines`/`MAX_LINES` have no callers or tests in the repo, confirmed by repo-wide grep).\n\n**Severity:** high (not critical only because there are no callers in-repo, so no reachable data-loss path is demonstrated; any future caller passing user-controlled content turns this into a panic on untrusted input).\n\n**Smallest correct fix:** bound the iteration by both the limit and the slice length:\n\n```rust\npub fn first_lines(lines: &[&str]) -> Vec<String> {\n    (0..MAX_LINES.min(lines.len())).map(|i| lines[i].to_string()).collect()\n}\n```\n\n(equivalently `lines.iter().take(MAX_LINES).map(|s| s.to_string()).collect()`)\n\n### 2. Off-by-one: returns `MAX_LINES + 1` elements, not `MAX_LINES` — **high**\n\n**Evidence:** `src/limit.rs:3` — `(0..=MAX_LINES)` with `MAX_LINES = 3` produces 4 elements.\n\n**Failure condition:** for any input with `len >= 4`, e.g. `[\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"d\"]`, the function returns `[\"a\", \"b\", \"c\", \"d\"]` — 4 lines — despite `MAX_LINES = 3` and a function named `first_lines`. The inclusive bound `..=` contradicts the constant's name/value and the function's implied contract (a maximum of `MAX_LINES` lines). This is a contract/behavior mismatch between names/docs and actual behavior: `MAX_LINES` is effectively treated as an exclusive bound / \"index of last line\", not a maximum count.\n\n**Wrong result:** every caller relying on \"at most 3 lines\" gets 4; the returned `Vec` length violates the stated maximum.\n\n**Smallest correct fix:** same one-line change as finding 1 — replace `0..=MAX_LINES` with `0..MAX_LINES.min(lines.len())` (or `.take(MAX_LINES)`). Both findings share this single root cause and one fix.\n\n*(If — contrary to the names — the intended semantics really were \"return lines 0..=3\", the correct fix would instead be renaming the constant to make the inclusive bound explicit; but the names `MAX_LINES`/`first_lines` make that reading unsupportable, and\n\n[956 bytes omitted from delegated task output]\n\nResult`/`Option`/`unsafe`/`unwrap`/`expect` and takes no other APIs. The only error path is the panic in finding 1.\n- **Ordering/race assumptions:** none — pure, single-threaded, no shared state, deterministic given input.\n\n## Could not verify\n\n- **Compile-level diagnostics:** `code_diagnostics` on `src/limit.rs` failed (`rust-analyzer reported warning health`), so I could not confirm a clean compile. Both findings are source-level and independent of that. The file is also not referenced by any module, so no crate target compiles it in this workspace as-is.\n- **Intended semantics from docs/tests:** there are no doc comments, tests, or callers to disambiguate intent; the severity of finding 2 rests on the constant's name (`MAX_LINES`) and the function name (`first_lines`) as the contract.", exit_code: 0, metadata: Some(